#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567647 :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,z)&=\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{}+\\left(6 y z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &{}+\\left(3 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=4 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=6 y z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=3 y^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(4 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=6 y z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=6 y z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int \\left(6 y z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=3 y^{2} z+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=3 y^{2} z+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=3 y^{2} z + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=3 y^{2}+h'(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=3 y^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 y^{2}+h'(z)&=3 y^{2} \\\\ h'(z)&=0 \\\\ h(z)&=C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=3 y^{2} z + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567650 :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,z)&=\\left(y e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{}+\\left(x e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &{}+\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(2 z \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=y e^{x y} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=x e^{x y} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=2 \\cos{\\left(2 z \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(y e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=e^{x y}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=x e^{x y}+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=x e^{x y}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x e^{x y}+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=x e^{x y} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int \\left(0\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=e^{x y}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=h'(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=2 \\cos{\\left(2 z \\right)}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h'(z)&=2 \\cos{\\left(2 z \\right)} \\\\ h(z)&=\\sin{\\left(2 z \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=e^{x y} + \\sin{\\left(2 z \\right)}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567663 :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,z)&=\\left(3 y^{2} z^{3} - e^{z}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{}+\\left(6 x y z^{3}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &{}+\\left(9 x y^{2} z^{2} - x e^{z}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=3 y^{2} z^{3} - e^{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=6 x y z^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=9 x y^{2} z^{2} - x e^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(3 y^{2} z^{3} - e^{z}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=3 x y^{2} z^{3} - x e^{z}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=6 x y z^{3}+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=6 x y z^{3}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 x y z^{3}+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=6 x y z^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int \\left(0\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=3 x y^{2} z^{3} - x e^{z}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=9 x y^{2} z^{2} - x e^{z}+h'(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=9 x y^{2} z^{2} - x e^{z}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 x y^{2} z^{2} - x e^{z}+h'(z)&=9 x y^{2} z^{2} - x e^{z} \\\\ h'(z)&=0 \\\\ h(z)&=C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=3 x y^{2} z^{3} - x e^{z}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567666 :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,z)&=\\left(- 2 y - 2\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{}+\\left(- 2 x + 3 z + 3\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &{}+\\left(3 y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=- 2 y - 2 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- 2 x + 3 z + 3 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=3 y + 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(- 2 y - 2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=- 2 x y - 2 x+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- 2 x+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- 2 x + 3 z + 3$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 2 x+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=- 2 x + 3 z + 3 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=3 z + 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int \\left(3 z + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=3 y z + 3 y+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=3 y z + 3 y+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- 2 x y - 2 x + 3 y z + 3 y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=3 y+h'(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=3 y + 3$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 y+h'(z)&=3 y + 3 \\\\ h'(z)&=3 \\\\ h(z)&=3 z+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- 2 x y - 2 x + 3 y z + 3 y + 3 z+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567671 :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,z)&=\\left(2 y^{2} z^{3} - 2 e^{z}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{}+\\left(4 x y z^{3}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &{}+\\left(6 x y^{2} z^{2} - 2 x e^{z}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=2 y^{2} z^{3} - 2 e^{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=4 x y z^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=6 x y^{2} z^{2} - 2 x e^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(2 y^{2} z^{3} - 2 e^{z}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=2 x y^{2} z^{3} - 2 x e^{z}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=4 x y z^{3}+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=4 x y z^{3}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 x y z^{3}+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=4 x y z^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int \\left(0\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2 x y^{2} z^{3} - 2 x e^{z}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=6 x y^{2} z^{2} - 2 x e^{z}+h'(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=6 x y^{2} z^{2} - 2 x e^{z}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 x y^{2} z^{2} - 2 x e^{z}+h'(z)&=6 x y^{2} z^{2} - 2 x e^{z} \\\\ h'(z)&=0 \\\\ h(z)&=C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2 x y^{2} z^{3} - 2 x e^{z}+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567685 :question/problem "![](images/19/1918cfc0e0cc83334a16c7ee544b7314a8d12054999ce7c175604a2a39eb63ac.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "![](images/50/50d98e45b9996b85e50191c2164348ef4492dd45623c5c072699f2dac2feb87e.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=1$ and $BH=5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(1)^2+(5)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1+25} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{26}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $a$ is $\\sqrt{26}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|a|}\\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{26}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567688 :question/problem "![](images/ad/ad06bc93ffe1e70bf9ca543588c14a1185f306ea19646559d11cc5adadb005bd.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=4x\\,\\mathbf i+y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/83/83bf612af65f14848f10293bea459e926cd8246c2c48ea223709becd7acc1b1b.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages.\n\n- First, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}(4x\\,\\mathbf i+y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf i\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=4\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=2\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf j,\\quad t\\in[-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=4\\int_{-2}^{2}2\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(8)\\int_{-2}^{2}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(8)\\cdot 4\\\\\n&=32.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds=32.\n$$\n\n- Next, consider the side $C_2.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_2=\\mathbf j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_2}(4x\\,\\mathbf i+y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf j\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+2\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf i,\\quad t\\in[-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{-2}^{2}2\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(2)\\int_{-2}^{2}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(2)\\cdot 4\\\\\n&=8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds=8.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=2\\cdot(32)+2\\cdot(8)=80.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567690 :question/problem "![](images/cf/cfe74c0d060535a5df1354feb7c154867f0e791b99ec92a0fed842b30891b2f4.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=4x\\,\\mathbf i-2y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/db/db373c1f110dae0f35d11c09099adc64a8aee266a2a65df5d68dd8faa673854a.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages.\n\n- First, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}(4x\\,\\mathbf i-2y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf i\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=4\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=1\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf j,\\quad t\\in[-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=4\\int_{-1}^{1}1\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(4)\\int_{-1}^{1}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(4)\\cdot 2\\\\\n&=8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds=8.\n$$\n\n- Next, consider the side $C_2.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_2=\\mathbf j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_2}(4x\\,\\mathbf i-2y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf j\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=-2\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+1\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf i,\\quad t\\in[-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=-2\\int_{-1}^{1}1\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(-2)\\int_{-1}^{1}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(-2)\\cdot 2\\\\\n&=-4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds=-4.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=2\\cdot(8)+2\\cdot(-4)=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567699 :question/problem "![](images/16/1622bef33dbaa424dcd7e19db6df88d38b0b50aba8b22675cf17876633ab4b95.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=-5x\\,\\mathbf i-4y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/db/db373c1f110dae0f35d11c09099adc64a8aee266a2a65df5d68dd8faa673854a.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages.\n\n- First, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}(-5x\\,\\mathbf i-4y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf i\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=-5\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=1\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf j,\\quad t\\in[-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=-5\\int_{-1}^{1}1\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(-5)\\int_{-1}^{1}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(-5)\\cdot 2\\\\\n&=-10.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds=-10.\n$$\n\n- Next, consider the side $C_2.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_2=\\mathbf j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_2}(-5x\\,\\mathbf i-4y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf j\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=-4\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+1\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf i,\\quad t\\in[-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=-4\\int_{-1}^{1}1\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(-4)\\int_{-1}^{1}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(-4)\\cdot 2\\\\\n&=-8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds=-8.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=2\\cdot(-10)+2\\cdot(-8)=-36.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567702 :question/problem "![](images/48/480f1467463e8003c8a862d20e4e9feca23f175be4381ef613985f49addec4b0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=3x\\,\\mathbf i+y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/11/1159164af4a9cacd4e06e3953e6323a6fdfad709cb0e0f69b9377c7a8ec528ff.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages.\n\n- First, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}(3x\\,\\mathbf i+y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf i\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=3\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=3\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf j,\\quad t\\in[-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=3\\int_{-3}^{3}3\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(9)\\int_{-3}^{3}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(9)\\cdot 6\\\\\n&=54.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds=54.\n$$\n\n- Next, consider the side $C_2.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_2=\\mathbf j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_2}(3x\\,\\mathbf i+y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf j\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+3\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf i,\\quad t\\in[-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{-3}^{3}3\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(3)\\int_{-3}^{3}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(3)\\cdot 6\\\\\n&=18.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds=18.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=2\\cdot(54)+2\\cdot(18)=144.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567704 :question/problem "![](images/c0/c03e6d49996327392dc747c513102a61fdb53603f7b8e3942499e8bf96aac401.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=4x\\,\\mathbf i+5y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/db/db373c1f110dae0f35d11c09099adc64a8aee266a2a65df5d68dd8faa673854a.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages.\n\n- First, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}(4x\\,\\mathbf i+5y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf i\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=4\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=1\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf j,\\quad t\\in[-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=4\\int_{-1}^{1}1\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(4)\\int_{-1}^{1}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(4)\\cdot 2\\\\\n&=8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds=8.\n$$\n\n- Next, consider the side $C_2.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_2=\\mathbf j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_2}(4x\\,\\mathbf i+5y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf j\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=5\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+1\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf i,\\quad t\\in[-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=5\\int_{-1}^{1}1\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(5)\\int_{-1}^{1}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(5)\\cdot 2\\\\\n&=10.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds=10.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=2\\cdot(8)+2\\cdot(10)=36.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570726 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,\\frac{1}{2},1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\frac{1}{y}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,\\frac{1}{2}) & =2+2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{1}{(1/2)} \\\\  & =2+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,\\frac{1}{2}) & ={f}_{y}(2,\\frac{1}{2})(y-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =4(y-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =4y-2 \\\\ z & =4y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,\\frac{1}{2},1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=4y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=4y-1$ (or $4y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,4,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,4,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j+4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,4⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571385 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\operatorname{log}⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n**Hint:** $\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)={log}_{10}⁡(x)$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x))}^{′}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x){(\\operatorname{log}⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =({sec}^{2}⁡(x))(\\operatorname{log}⁡(x))+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x))\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}\\right) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)+\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571432 :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=2x+1$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=1$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)+1}{1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571442 :question/problem "Differentiate ${x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={x}^{3}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({x}^{3})^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+({x}^{3})(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571450 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=({x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={x}^{2}+3$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({x}^{2}+3)}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+({x}^{2}+3){(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =(2x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))+({x}^{2}+3)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}+3}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572109 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{m}$ has magnitude $\\frac{7}{2}.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $m$, we divide $m$ by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{m}{|\\,m\\,|}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}m=\\frac{2}{7}m\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572119 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is directed due north-west and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|=5$, while the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is directed due south-west and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=12.$ Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}\\mathbf{+}\\mathbf{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $a+b$ goes from the tail of $a$ to the head of $b:$   \n\n![](images/15/153aff405a3ae0859ff74fcb98deb09e038db7b033cbe54223866c9fe1724fb1.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b,$ we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, first notice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{|a+b|}^{2} & ={|a|}^{2}+{|b|}^{2} \\\\  & ={5}^{2}+{12}^{2} \\\\  & =169\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{169}=13,\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(a+b).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572126 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $\\sqrt{3}$, while the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is directed vertically up and has magnitude $\\sqrt{6}.$ Find a unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ that is parallel to  $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ and has the same direction as $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}.$ \n\nType $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to represent the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ respectively.  \n\n$\\mathbf{u}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $a+b$ goes from the tail of $a$ to the head of $b:$ \n\n![](images/b7/b7d45d7cd1ea2c61961da8279d3f8702d3e52092fc6aab75a94d3b5076928dba.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b,$ we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, first notice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{|a+b|}^{2} & ={|a|}^{2}+{|b|}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =3+6 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{9}=3,\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(a+b).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572132 :question/problem "The vector $\\overset{\\to }{ST}$ has magnitude $6.$ Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overset{\\to }{ST}?$" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overset{\\to }{ST},$ we divide $\\overset{\\to }{ST}$ by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{ST}}{|\\overset{\\to }{ST}|}=\\frac{1}{6}\\overset{\\to }{ST}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572134 :question/problem "![](images/31/314cd1d2e150c078fa7232c81125de44fb8c26c176d2a1f32ab02fbae2eef299.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{m}=-\\mathbf{g}$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{z}$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{g}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{m}$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. The magnitude of $m$ is greater than the magnitude of $g.$ So, $m\\ne -g.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The vectors $a$ and $z$ have opposite directions. So, $a\\ne z.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, vectors $g$ and $m$ are parallel and have opposite directions. Also the magnitude of $g$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ times the magnitude of $m.$ So, $g=-\\frac{1}{2}m.$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572138 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is directed due north-east and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|=7$, while the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is directed due north-west and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=7.$ Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}\\mathbf{+}\\mathbf{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $a+b$ goes from the tail of $a$ to the head of $b:$   \n\n![](images/94/9468530a650f0bee117a4254aa87b5954c7bbbe2684d95f9db3f9961026abcb2.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b,$ we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, first notice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{|a+b|}^{2} & ={|a|}^{2}+{|b|}^{2} \\\\  & ={7}^{2}+{7}^{2} \\\\  & =98\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{98}=7\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\sqrt{2}}(a+b).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572150 :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+24}$ at the point where $x=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3{x}^{2}+24.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 6x \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+24}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting  $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-2} & ={\\left(\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+24}}\\right)|}_{x=-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6)}{\\sqrt{3(-2)^{2}+24}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{6} \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572153 :question/problem "![](images/b2/b2c3f59df41cb406ddfc41eea81a979dec578ba990032f0ff4f2f9792f58c8b2.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $-\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{a}$ and $2\\mathbf{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $-\\frac{3}{4}a$ and $2a$ must be parallel to $a.$\n\nNow, for the vector $-\\frac{3}{4}a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{3}{4}a$ is $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the magnitude of $a.$\n\n- Vectors $a$ and $-\\frac{3}{4}a$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{3}{4}$ is negative.\n\n![](images/13/13e4de227344b9eb7d1867cca0d05d9bcb647662a0c732398b42277ac379d361.png)\n\n \n\nNext, for the vector $2a$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2a$ is twice the magnitude of $a.$\n\n- Vectors $a$ and $2a$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is the following.\n\n![](images/13/13ca18bebfdb3c738d0a1198d0ce4235bfd77185bcf232f79f9ba089ce3b6d38.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572156 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{b}$ has magnitude $20.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $b$, we divide $b$ by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{b}{|\\,b\\,|}=\\frac{1}{20}b\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572158 :question/problem "![](images/60/605c5def8bc643f0f601f89b9f8d7b556ac94206867dbe8904ef746c83f02fc0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the vector $\\mathbf{a}.$ Which of the following diagrams shows the vector $\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $\\frac{3}{2}a$ must be parallel to $a.$\nNow, we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $\\frac{3}{2}a$ is $\\frac{3}{2}$ of the magnitude of $a.$\n\n- Vectors $a$ and $\\frac{3}{2}a$ have the same direction since the factor $\\frac{3}{2}$ is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is the following.\n\n![](images/59/591682cfbe0f50347d88f894f4c2b740fca140e8a93838f5c53022f9f50ee6da.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572160 :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda (4\\mathbf{a}+\\sqrt{7}\\mathbf{b})$ and $4\\sqrt{7}\\,\\mathbf{a}+7\\mathbf{b}$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $\\lambda .$\n\n$\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since the vectors are equal, we can write\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt{7}\\,a+7b=\\lambda (4a+\\sqrt{7}b).\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the left-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{7}(4a+\\sqrt{7}b)=\\lambda (4a+\\sqrt{7}b).\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $(4a+\\sqrt{7}b)$ on both sides. So, let's define a new vector $u$ as\n\n$$\nu=4a+\\sqrt{7}b.\n$$\n\nThen, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{7}\\,u=\\lambda u.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =\\sqrt{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572161 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3})}^{2},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3})^{′}=4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot (4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3})(4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572164 :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda (12\\mathbf{a}-52\\mathbf{b})$ and $-3\\mathbf{a}+13\\mathbf{b}$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "Since the vectors are equal, we can write\n\n$$\n-3a+13b=\\lambda (12a-52b).\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the left-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{4}(12a-52b)=\\lambda (12a-52b).\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $(12a-52b)$ on both sides. So, let's define a new vector $u$ as\n\n$$\nu=12a-52b.\n$$\n\nThen, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{4}u=\\lambda u.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572174 :question/problem "![](images/13/13fc8ff564a7162c556d050a10df6e8f23c0fca269e3b88168ca6681be3ad3a3.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Find a unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ that is parallel to  $\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}$ and has the same direction as $\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}.$ \n\nType $\\mathbf{p}$ and $q$ to represent the vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q},$ respectively.  \n\n$\\mathbf{u}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $p+q.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $p+q$ goes from the tail of $p$ to the head of $q:$ \n\n![](images/68/68c14ce5ff66272986e958f09f30289d56519e90b34fae23362753f84c042a50.png)\n\nIn $△AHB$, we have $∠AHB={90}^{∘}$, $AH=2$ and $BH=3.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|p+q| & =AB \\\\  & =\\sqrt{A{H}^{2}+B{H}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $p+q$ is $\\sqrt{13}.$ Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{1}{|p+q|}\\cdot (p+q) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}(p+q).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572179 :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa72f6ea8c52a1f141f8469c3f932937b8b957956dc478811910e77c83336a9c.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{p}?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/4d/4d4d7faa0c0611c8ef891cda5fef3b75a61aa97e0879fac525f58f4ce263c937.png)\n\nIn $△AHB$, we have $∠AHB={90}^{∘}$, $AH=2$ and $BH=5.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{A{H}^{2}+B{H}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $p$ is $\\sqrt{29}.$ Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{1}{|\\,p\\,|}\\cdot p \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{29}}p.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572194 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x-{x}^{2}}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=x-{x}^{2}$ and $f(g(x))=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{g}}=-4{g}^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =-4({g}^{-1/2})^{′}=4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=\\frac{2}{{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(x-{x}^{2})^{′}=1-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (1-2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2-4x}{\\sqrt{(x-{x}^{2})^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572196 :question/problem "The vector $\\overset{\\to }{UV}$ has magnitude $\\frac{1}{3}.$ Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overset{\\to }{UV}?$" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overset{\\to }{UV},$ we divide $\\overset{\\to }{UV}$ by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{UV}}{|\\overset{\\to }{UV}|}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=3\\overset{\\to }{UV}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572198 :question/problem "![](images/92/9236e136f26433023d806181fcfbef9c05d4976f75e14f74840ae7f48a35da99.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{r}=3\\mathbf{q}$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{s}=2\\mathbf{t}$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{p}=-\\mathbf{s}$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. The vectors $r$ and $q$ have the opposite directions. So, $r\\ne 3q.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, vectors $s$ and $t$ are parallel and have the same directions. Also, the magnitude of $s$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $t.$ So, $s=2t.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The vectors $p$ and $s$ are parallel and have the opposite directions. Also, their magnitudes are the same. So, $p=-s.$\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, and statement I is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572664 :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(0,2,-1),$ $B(3,4,1),$ $C(-1,3,2),$ and $D(2,1,0).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i+4j+k)-(2j-k) \\\\  & =3i+2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨3,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-i+3j+2k)-(2j-k) \\\\  & =-i+j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(2i+j)-(2j-k) \\\\  & =2i-j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot (-1))-2((-1)\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2)+2((-1)\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =3(1+3)-2(-1-6)+2(1-2) \\\\  & =12-(-14)-2 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|24|=24.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572689 :question/problem "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{p}=⟨1,0,5⟩$, $\\mathbf{q}=⟨3,2,6⟩$, and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨0,4,1⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The volume of a parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(2\\cdot 1-6\\cdot 4)+0+5(3\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-22+60 \\\\  & =38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|38|=38.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572715 :question/problem "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{x}=⟨2,3,0⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨1,4,5⟩$, and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨0,2,6⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The volume of a parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(4\\cdot 6-5\\cdot 2)-3(1\\cdot 6-5\\cdot 0)+0 \\\\  & =2(24-10)-3(6) \\\\  & =28-18 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|10|=10.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572726 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨k,k,2⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,3,0⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨0,4,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & k & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k(3\\cdot 5-0\\cdot 4)-k(1\\cdot 5-0\\cdot 0)+2(1\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =k(15)-k(5)+2(4) \\\\  & =15k-5k+8 \\\\  & =10k+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 10k+8 & =0 \\\\ 10k & =-8 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{4}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573262 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{a}=3\\mathbf{i}-5\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-4\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j},$ then $\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}=${{field-1}} $\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.167em}-$\n{{field-2}} $\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b},$ we just need to compute all of the $\\mathbf{i}$'s and $\\mathbf{j}$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b} & =(3\\mathbf{i}-5\\mathbf{j})-(-4\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-5\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{i}-7\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{i})+(-5\\mathbf{j}-7\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =7\\mathbf{i}-12\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573264 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2,-1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-1,2⟩$, then $\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}=$$\\,⟨$\n{{field-1}}$\\,,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b} & =⟨2,-1⟩-⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-(-1),-1-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
